yoyo now warns about config keys it never reads. With modle = "gpt-4o" in .yoyo.toml, every run prints this on stderr and then carries on:

```
warning: .yoyo.toml line 4: `modle` is not a key yoyo reads, so it has no effect; did you mean `model`?
```

Format, clippy and cargo test all pass (7414 tests, 0 failed). It's committed as 2b59935c, with "Fixes #1171" in the body, so the push will close #1171 (the issue I filed at the start).

How it behaves
• It warns and never refuses, so a key from a newer yoyo or one your own tools read won't stop the run.
• Each problem gets one line, printed once per run, on stderr only. In a test run, stdout had none of these lines.
• A misspelled section such as [permisions] gets one line listing the keys it hides, with a suggestion of [permissions].

Where the list of known keys came from: I built it by searching the code that reads the config for every name it looks up, not from /config set's list. That gives 30 top-level keys, hooks.* keys, and the keys of the four sections yoyo reads. One of the 30, audit, only turned up in a search that also catches calls split across lines; I would have missed it writing the list from memory. A test re-runs that search, so a new config key added to the code but not to the list fails by name. I checked it by removing audit: the test failed naming ("cli.rs", "audit"), and passed again once I put it back.

Two existing silent failures it now reports:
• A [hooks] table is never read. Hooks have to be written as top-level dotted keys like hooks.pre.bash.
• A comment after a section header, as in [permissions] # mine, means the line isn't recognised as a header. The allow underneath it was silently ignored; it's now reported.

Where it's wired in: in the normal config loading path, and also in config get and config show, which read the file on their own. My first config get model test printed nothing until I added those two. The repo's own .yoyo.toml gives no warnings, and there's a test for that.

Filed, not fixed: #1172. With model = "", config get reports the key as set, but the run ignores it and uses the default model (gpt-6-sol with provider openai). I confirmed this on the built binary.

I also added a sentence to the config section of the install guide, a CHANGELOG entry under Unreleased, and an ARCHITECTURE.md entry for the new file src/config_unknown_keys.rs. The tests/module_size.rs entry for config.rs is updated to its measured size.

